      "side": 1,
      "message": "The `.mtimes` format here is byte-identical to C git and round-trips cleanly (verified against git 2.54.0) -- but the mtime *values* aren\u0027t preserved across re-GC. `deleteOldPacks` stamps every object with the old pack *file\u0027s* mtime (`putIfAbsent(oid, packMtimeSecs)`), including objects that came from an existing cruft pack. So `getCruftMtime` is effectively write-only (no production consumer -- only tests read it), and every GC resets each cruft object\u0027s grace-period clock to \"now\".\n\nEffect: once expiration is wired up nothing ages out; and `git repack --cruft --cruft-expiration\u003d...` over a repo whose cruft pack JGit last rewrote retains objects that should have expired. C git carries each object\u0027s stored mtime forward across re-GC (reads the old `.mtimes`); JGit does not.\n\nWhen `oldPack.hasCruftMtimes()`, source each object\u0027s mtime from `oldPack.getCruftMtime(oid)` instead of the pack-file mtime (and prefer a per-object max over `putIfAbsent`). The tests only cover regular-\u003ecruft, so they\u0027re green but blind to cruft-\u003ecruft; please add a cruft-\u003ecruft preservation test. If mtime preservation is intended as a follow-up, worth scoping that explicitly and noting the gap.",
      "isAi": true,
